Now I see the key insight. In `MqttClient_WaitType`:

```c
/* Decode Packet - get type, qos and id */
rc = MqttClient_DecodePacket(client, client->rx_buf,
    client->packet.buf_len, NULL, &packet_type, &packet_qos,
    &packet_id, 1);
if (rc < 0) {
    break;
}

MqttClient_PacketReset(packet_type, &client->msg);
```

The first `MqttClient_DecodePacket` with `packet_obj = NULL` uses a local zeroed struct. It determines the packet type. Then `MqttClient_PacketReset(packet_type, &client->msg)` zeroes `client->msg`.

But then:
```c
if ((wait_type == MQTT_PACKET_TYPE_ANY ||
     wait_type == packet_type ||
     (MqttIsPubRespPacket(packet_type) &&
      MqttIsPubRespPacket(wait_type))) &&
    (wait_packet_id == 0 || wait_packet_id == packet_id))
{
    use_packet_obj = packet_obj;
    ...
}
else {
    /* use generic packet object */
    use_packet_obj = &client->msg;
    ...
}
```

So `use_packet_obj` is either `packet_obj` (the caller's struct) or `&client->msg` (the shared struct).

For `MqttClient_Subscribe`, `packet_obj = &subscribe->ack`. This was zeroed when `subscribe` was memset. So `props` is NULL.

For `MqttClient_WaitMessage`, `packet_obj = &client->msg`. This was zeroed by `MqttClient_PacketReset`. So `props` is NULL.

